
===== src/serial_control/launch/serial.launch.py (36 lines)
from launch import LaunchDescription
from launch.actions import DeclareLaunchArgument
from launch.substitutions import LaunchConfiguration
from launch_ros.actions import Node


def generate_launch_description():
    return LaunchDescription([
        DeclareLaunchArgument(
            'device',
            default_value='/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Serial-if00-port0',
            description='Serial device path.'),
        DeclareLaunchArgument(
            'baud', default_value='115200',
            description='Baud rate.'),
        DeclareLaunchArgument(
            'accel_device',
            default_value='/dev/serial/by-id/'
                          'usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_A5069RR4-if00-port0',
            description='Accelerator board serial device path.'),
        DeclareLaunchArgument(
            'accel_baud', default_value='115200',
            description='Accelerator board baud rate.'),
        Node(
            package='serial_control',
            executable='cmd',
            name='cmd',
            output='screen',
            parameters=[{
                'device': LaunchConfiguration('device'),
                'baud': LaunchConfiguration('baud'),
                'accel_device': LaunchConfiguration('accel_device'),
                'accel_baud': LaunchConfiguration('accel_baud'),
            }],
        ),
    ])

===== src/serial_control/serial_control/cmd_node.py (475 lines)
"""ROS 2 node "cmd": bridge between the /cmd namespace and a serial device.

Python port of the Go websocket<->serial bridge, recast onto the existing
web_server plumbing (which already owns the socket, auth and namespaces).
The device is the ARGUS DRIVE controller (firmware v4.1.3): each console
token is translated to hex-text frames by argus_protocol; tokens outside its
vocabulary are logged and never reach the wire. On every (re)connect the
node sends INFO (0x01) so the firmware version shows up in the log.

Steering feedback: the node polls READ:STEER (0x04) at steer_poll_hz and
reads the steering limits (0x10) on connect. The angle goes out as degrees
from the centre of the limits (the point STEER:C centres to), as a
"TELEM steer=<deg>" line the console's parseTelemetry already understands.
Poll traffic is logged at debug level and not echoed raw to the browser.

Brake feedback works the same way: READ:BRAKE (0x03) is polled with the
steering, the brake limits (0x0F) are read on connect, and the angle goes
out as "TELEM brake=<0-100 %> brakedeg=<deg>" (brake= only once the limits
are known). 0 % is the min limit; brake_invert flips it.

Throttle lives on a second serial device, the accelerator board
(accel_protocol). ACCEL tokens go only there; everything else goes to ARGUS.
The node enforces the brake interlock the ARGUS firmware used to: BRAKE:APPLY
and ESTOP send duty 0 to the accel board first, and ACCEL is refused while
the brake is applied. The accel board also gets duty 0 on its own (re)connect
and whenever the drive link drops, since the brake is then unreachable.

The board resets when its port opens, so the node waits accel_boot_s before
the first frames (duty 0, INFO). After that every frame must be answered: a
USB-serial link can stall (kernel "urb stopped: -32") while writes still
"succeed" on the host. No reply within accel_reply_timeout_s raises
ACCEL_LINK and reopens the port. Throttle above 0 is refused
("0x02;ERR;ACCEL_NOT_READY") until the board has answered since connecting.

  /web_server/cmd/out  (browser -> here)   tokens, encoded and written
  /web_server/cmd/in   (here -> browser)   serial lines + TELEM steer=
  ~/rx                                     serial lines for ROS consumers
  ~/status                                 drive link state JSON
  ~/accel_status                           accel link state JSON
  ~/steer_angle        Float32             absolute steering angle, deg
  ~/steer              Float32             deg from centre (+ per steer_invert)
  ~/brake_angle        Float32             absolute brake actuator angle, deg
  ~/brake              Float32             brake travel 0-100 % of the limits

Alert system (same JSON shape as bms_monitor, rendered by the same UI):
  - SERIAL_LINK   critical on link loss, cleared on reconnect
  - ACCEL_LINK    same, for the accelerator board
  - SERIAL_WRITE  warning when a command can't be written (edge-triggered,
                  cleared by the next successful write); ACCEL_WRITE likewise
  - DEVICE_MSG    warning for device lines matching alert_patterns
                  (default: ERR, FAULT, ALERT)
"""

import json
import threading
import time

import rclpy
from rclpy.node import Node
from rclpy.qos import (QoSDurabilityPolicy, QoSProfile,
                       QoSReliabilityPolicy)
from std_msgs.msg import Float32, String

from serial_control import accel_protocol, argus_protocol
from serial_control.serial_link import DEFAULT_BAUD, DEFAULT_DEVICE, SerialLink

STEER_REPLY = '0x04'
LIMITS_REPLY = '0x10'
BRAKE_REPLY = '0x03'
BRAKE_LIMITS_REPLY = '0x0F'

# FT232R adapters carry a unique serial, so by-id stays stable even next to
# the CH340 (whose by-id name has no serial and would collide with a twin).
ACCEL_DEVICE = '/dev/serial/by-id/usb-FTDI_FT232R_USB_UART_A5069RR4-if00-port0'

# Latched so the status checker can read the current link state on demand.
LATCHED = QoSProfile(
    depth=1,
    reliability=QoSReliabilityPolicy.RELIABLE,
    durability=QoSDurabilityPolicy.TRANSIENT_LOCAL)


class CmdNode(Node):

    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__('cmd')
        self.declare_parameter('device', DEFAULT_DEVICE)
        self.declare_parameter('baud', DEFAULT_BAUD)
        self.declare_parameter('alert_patterns', ['ERR', 'FAULT', 'ALERT'])
        # 0 disables polling. Each poll is one short read-only line.
        self.declare_parameter('steer_poll_hz', 5.0)
        # flip the sign of ~/steer if the angle grows towards the left
        self.declare_parameter('steer_invert', False)
        # flip ~/brake if the angle shrinks as the brake applies
        self.declare_parameter('brake_invert', False)
        self.declare_parameter('accel_device', ACCEL_DEVICE)
        self.declare_parameter('accel_baud', DEFAULT_BAUD)
        self.declare_parameter('accel_max_pct', accel_protocol.DEFAULT_MAX_PCT)
        # the board reboots on port open; nothing sent to it lands until then
        self.declare_parameter('accel_boot_s', 2.5)
        self.declare_parameter('accel_reply_timeout_s', 1.0)
        device = str(self.get_parameter('device').value)
        baud = int(self.get_parameter('baud').value)
        self.patterns = [str(p).upper()
                         for p in self.get_parameter('alert_patterns').value]
        poll_hz = float(self.get_parameter('steer_poll_hz').value)
        self.steer_invert = bool(self.get_parameter('steer_invert').value)
        self.brake_invert = bool(self.get_parameter('brake_invert').value)
        accel_device = str(self.get_parameter('accel_device').value)
        accel_baud = int(self.get_parameter('accel_baud').value)
        self.accel_max_pct = int(self.get_parameter('accel_max_pct').value)
        if not 0 <= self.accel_max_pct <= 100:
            raise ValueError('accel_max_pct must be 0-100, got %d'
                             % self.accel_max_pct)

        self.rx_pub = self.create_publisher(String, '~/rx', 10)
        self.status_pub = self.create_publisher(String, '~/status', LATCHED)
        self.accel_status_pub = self.create_publisher(
            String, '~/accel_status', LATCHED)
        self.web_cmd_pub = self.create_publisher(
            String, '/web_server/cmd/in', 10)
        self.web_alert_pub = self.create_publisher(
            String, '/web_server/alert/in', 10)
        self.alerts_pub = self.create_publisher(String, '~/alerts', 10)
        self.steer_angle_pub = self.create_publisher(
            Float32, '~/steer_angle', 10)
        self.steer_pub = self.create_publisher(Float32, '~/steer', 10)
        self.brake_angle_pub = self.create_publisher(
            Float32, '~/brake_angle', 10)
        self.brake_pub = self.create_publisher(Float32, '~/brake', 10)

        self.create_subscription(
            String, '/web_server/cmd/out', self.on_command, 10)

        self._write_failed = {}
        self._brake_on = False
        # Serialises throttle decisions (executor thread) against the drive
        # reader thread zeroing the throttle on link loss, so an ACCEL that
        # passed the link check cannot land after that zero.
        self._throttle_lock = threading.Lock()
        # accel board liveness: hello is due at _accel_hello_at after a
        # connect; _accel_alive once it has answered since then
        self._accel_boot_s = float(self.get_parameter('accel_boot_s').value)
        self._accel_watch = accel_protocol.AckWatch(float(
            self.get_parameter('accel_reply_timeout_s').value))
        self._accel_hello_at = None
        self._accel_hello_sent = False
        self._accel_alive = False
        self.create_timer(0.25, self._accel_tick)
        self._steer_limits = None
        self._steer_error = False
        self._steer_frame = argus_protocol.encode('READ:STEER')[0]
        self._limits_frame = argus_protocol.encode('READ:STRLIM')[0]
        self._brake_limits = None
        self._brake_error = False
        self._brake_frame = argus_protocol.encode('READ:BRAKE')[0]
        self._brake_limits_frame = argus_protocol.encode('READ:BRKLIM')[0]
        self._polls = 0
        if poll_hz > 0:
            self.create_timer(1.0 / poll_hz, self.poll_steer)
        self.link = SerialLink(device=device, baud=baud,
                               on_line=self.on_line,
                               on_status=self.on_status,
                               decode=argus_protocol.format_reply)
        self.accel_link = SerialLink(device=accel_device, baud=accel_baud,
                                     on_line=self.on_accel_line,
                                     on_status=self.on_accel_status,
                                     decode=argus_protocol.format_reply)
        self.get_logger().info(
            'bridging %s @ %d baud <-> /cmd namespace' % (device, baud))
        self.get_logger().info(
            'throttle on %s @ %d baud, capped at %d%%'
            % (accel_device, accel_baud, self.accel_max_pct))
        # publish the initial state so the latched topics are never empty
        self.status_pub.publish(String(data=json.dumps(
            {'connected': False,
             'message': 'waiting for %s' % device})))
        self.accel_status_pub.publish(String(data=json.dumps(
            {'connected': False,
             'message': 'waiting for %s' % accel_device})))
        self.link.start()
        self.accel_link.start()

    # -- browser -> serial ---------------------------------------------------

    def on_command(self, msg):
        with self._throttle_lock:
            self._command(msg)

    def _command(self, msg):
        drive_up = self.link.connected
        try:
            r = accel_protocol.route(msg.data, self._brake_on,
                                     max_pct=self.accel_max_pct,
                                     drive_up=drive_up)
        except accel_protocol.ThrottleLocked as e:
            self.get_logger().warning(str(e))
            # same shape the ARGUS firmware used for this refusal
            self.web_cmd_pub.publish(String(
                data='0x02;ERR;' + ('BRAKE_ACTIVE' if drive_up
                                    else 'DRIVE_LINK_DOWN')))
            return
        except (argus_protocol.UnknownCommand, ValueError) as e:
            self.get_logger().warning('ignored command: %s' % e)
            return
        if r.brake_on:
            # engaging locks the throttle even if the brake frame is lost
            self._brake_on = True
        if (r.accel and not self._accel_alive
                and any(f != accel_protocol.ZERO for f in r.accel)):
            self.get_logger().warning(
                '%s refused: accelerator board not answering' % msg.data)
            self.web_cmd_pub.publish(String(data='0x02;ERR;ACCEL_NOT_READY'))
            return
        if not r.main and not r.accel:
            self.get_logger().debug(
                'console-only command %r, nothing sent' % msg.data)
            return
        # throttle off before the brake moves
        if r.accel:
            self._send(self.accel_link, r.accel, 'ACCEL_WRITE', 'accel',
                       msg.data)
        if r.main and self._send(self.link, r.main, 'SERIAL_WRITE', 'serial',
                                 msg.data):
            self._brake_on = r.brake_on

    def _send(self, link, frames, code, name, token):
        """Write frames to one link, with edge-triggered write alerts."""
        if all(link.write_bytes(f) for f in frames):
            if link is self.accel_link:
                self._accel_sent()
            self.get_logger().info('%s <- %s [%s]'
                                   % (name, token,
                                      argus_protocol.describe(frames)))
            if self._write_failed.get(code):
                self._write_failed[code] = False
                self._alert('clear', code, 'info',
                            '%s writes working again' % name)
            return True
        self.get_logger().warning('%s dropped command %r (no link)'
                                  % (name, token))
        if not self._write_failed.get(code):
            self._write_failed[code] = True
            self._alert('trigger', code, 'warning',
                        'command %r dropped: %s device not connected'
                        % (token, name))
        return False

    # -- steering feedback ------------------------------------------------

    def poll_steer(self):
        if not self.link.connected:
            return
        self.link.write_bytes(self._steer_frame)
        self.link.write_bytes(self._brake_frame)
        # limits are read on connect; retry about once a second until known
        self._polls += 1
        if self._polls % 5 == 0:
            if self._steer_limits is None:
                self.link.write_bytes(self._limits_frame)
            if self._brake_limits is None:
                self.link.write_bytes(self._brake_limits_frame)

    def on_steer_reply(self, status, data):
        if status != 'OK':
            if not self._steer_error:
                self._steer_error = True
                self.get_logger().warning(
                    'steering angle read failed: %s' % data)
            return
        angle = argus_protocol.steer_angle_deg(data)
        if angle is None:
            return
        if self._steer_error:
            self._steer_error = False
            self.get_logger().info('steering angle readable again')
        self.steer_angle_pub.publish(Float32(data=angle))
        rel = argus_protocol.relative_steer(
            angle, self._steer_limits, invert=self.steer_invert)
        if rel is not None:
            self.steer_pub.publish(Float32(data=rel))
            self.web_cmd_pub.publish(String(data='TELEM steer=%.1f' % rel))

    def on_limits_reply(self, status, data):
        limits = argus_protocol.steer_limits_deg(data) if status == 'OK' else None
        if limits and limits != self._steer_limits:
            self._steer_limits = limits
            self.get_logger().info(
                'steering limits %.1f..%.1f deg, centre %.1f'
                % (limits[0], limits[1], sum(limits) / 2.0))

    def on_brake_reply(self, status, data):
        if status != 'OK':
            if not self._brake_error:
                self._brake_error = True
                self.get_logger().warning(
                    'brake feedback read failed: %s' % data)
            return
        angle = argus_protocol.brake_angle_deg(data)
        if angle is None:
            return
        if self._brake_error:
            self._brake_error = False
            self.get_logger().info('brake feedback readable again')
        self.brake_angle_pub.publish(Float32(data=angle))
        pct = argus_protocol.brake_percent(
            angle, self._brake_limits, invert=self.brake_invert)
        telem = 'TELEM brakedeg=%.1f' % angle
        if pct is not None:
            self.brake_pub.publish(Float32(data=pct))
            telem = 'TELEM brake=%.0f brakedeg=%.1f' % (pct, angle)
        self.web_cmd_pub.publish(String(data=telem))

    def on_brake_limits_reply(self, status, data):
        limits = (argus_protocol.brake_limits_deg(data)
                  if status == 'OK' else None)
        if limits and limits != self._brake_limits:
            self._brake_limits = limits
            self.get_logger().info('brake limits %.1f..%.1f deg' % limits)

    # -- serial -> browser/ROS -------------------------------------------------

    def on_line(self, line):
        reply = argus_protocol.parse_reply(line)
        if reply and reply[0].upper() == STEER_REPLY.upper():
            # 5 Hz poll traffic: keep it out of the log and the browser
            self.get_logger().debug('serial -> %r' % line)
            self.rx_pub.publish(String(data=line))
            self.on_steer_reply(reply[1], reply[2])
            return
        if reply and reply[0].upper() == BRAKE_REPLY.upper():
            self.get_logger().debug('serial -> %r' % line)
            self.rx_pub.publish(String(data=line))
            self.on_brake_reply(reply[1], reply[2])
            return
        self.get_logger().info('serial -> %r' % line)
        self.rx_pub.publish(String(data=line))
        self.web_cmd_pub.publish(String(data=line))
        if reply and reply[0].upper() == LIMITS_REPLY.upper():
            self.on_limits_reply(reply[1], reply[2])
        if reply and reply[0].upper() == BRAKE_LIMITS_REPLY.upper():
            self.on_brake_limits_reply(reply[1], reply[2])
        if reply and reply[0].lower() == '0x01' and reply[1] == 'OK':
            version = argus_protocol.firmware_version(reply[2])
            if version:
                self.get_logger().info('ARGUS DRIVE firmware v%s' % version)
        upper = line.upper()
        for pattern in self.patterns:
            if pattern in upper:
                self._alert('trigger', 'DEVICE_MSG', 'warning',
                            'device: %s' % line)
                break

    def on_status(self, connected, message):
        # rclpy loggers cache severity per call site — one shared log(msg)
        # line for both severities raises ValueError, so branch explicitly.
        if connected:
            self.get_logger().info(message)
        else:
            self.get_logger().error(message)
        self.status_pub.publish(String(data=json.dumps(
            {'connected': connected, 'message': message})))
        if connected:
            self._alert('clear', 'SERIAL_LINK', 'info', message)
            self.link.write_bytes(argus_protocol.encode('INFO')[0])
            self.link.write_bytes(self._limits_frame)
            self.link.write_bytes(self._brake_limits_frame)
        else:
            # the brake is unreachable now, so take the throttle off
            with self._throttle_lock:
                if self.accel_link.write_bytes(accel_protocol.ZERO):
                    self._accel_sent()
            self._alert('trigger', 'SERIAL_LINK', 'critical',
                        'serial link down: %s' % message)

    # -- accelerator board ----------------------------------------------------

    def _accel_sent(self):
        # only once the board has booted: frames sent into its reboot are
        # expected to go unanswered
        if self._accel_hello_sent:
            self._accel_watch.sent(time.monotonic())

    def _accel_tick(self):
        now = time.monotonic()
        if (self._accel_hello_at is not None and not self._accel_hello_sent
                and now >= self._accel_hello_at):
            self._accel_hello_sent = True
            # start from a known-safe duty, whatever the board held before
            for frame in (accel_protocol.ZERO, accel_protocol.INFO):
                if self.accel_link.write_bytes(frame):
                    self._accel_sent()
            return
        if self._accel_watch.dead(now):
            self._accel_watch.reset()
            self._accel_alive = False
            self.get_logger().error(
                'accelerator board not answering - reopening %s'
                % self.accel_link.device)
            self._alert('trigger', 'ACCEL_LINK', 'critical',
                        'accelerator board not answering (USB link stalled?)'
                        ' - reopening; replug its USB cable if this repeats')
            self.accel_link.reset('no reply')

    def on_accel_line(self, line):
        self.get_logger().info('accel -> %r' % line)
        self._accel_watch.replied(time.monotonic())
        if not self._accel_alive:
            self._accel_alive = True
            self._alert('clear', 'ACCEL_LINK', 'info',
                        'accelerator board answering')
        self.rx_pub.publish(String(data=line))
        self.web_cmd_pub.publish(String(data=line))
        reply = argus_protocol.parse_reply(line)
        if reply and reply[0].lower() == '0x01' and reply[1] == 'OK':
            version = accel_protocol.firmware_version(reply[2])
            if version:
                self.get_logger().info('accelerator firmware v%s' % version)
        upper = line.upper()
        for pattern in self.patterns:
            if pattern in upper:
                self._alert('trigger', 'DEVICE_MSG', 'warning',
                            'accel: %s' % line)
                break

    def on_accel_status(self, connected, message):
        if connected:
            self.get_logger().info('accel: %s' % message)
        else:
            self.get_logger().error('accel: %s' % message)
        self.accel_status_pub.publish(String(data=json.dumps(
            {'connected': connected, 'message': message})))
        self._accel_watch.reset()
        self._accel_alive = False
        self._accel_hello_sent = False
        if connected:
            # ACCEL_LINK clears on the board's first reply, not here: an
            # open port says nothing about whether frames arrive
            self._accel_hello_at = time.monotonic() + self._accel_boot_s
        else:
            self._accel_hello_at = None
            self._alert('trigger', 'ACCEL_LINK', 'critical',
                        'accelerator link down: %s' % message)

    # -- alerts ---------------------------------------------------------------

    def _alert(self, event, code, level, message):
        raw = json.dumps({'event': event, 'code': code, 'level': level,
                          'message': message, 'value': None, 'limit': None,
                          'ts': time.time() * 1000.0})
        self.alerts_pub.publish(String(data=raw))
        self.web_alert_pub.publish(String(data=raw))


def main(args=None):
    rclpy.init(args=args)
    node = CmdNode()
    try:
        rclpy.spin(node)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        pass
    finally:
        # the board latches its last duty, so zero it before letting go
        with node._throttle_lock:
            node.accel_link.write_bytes(accel_protocol.ZERO)
        node.accel_link.stop()
        node.link.stop()
        node.destroy_node()
        # Ctrl-C: rclpy's own signal handler has already shut down
        if rclpy.ok():
            rclpy.shutdown()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()

===== src/serial_control/serial_control/serial_link.py (147 lines)
"""Serial device link with automatic reconnect.

(Named serial_link, not serial — a module called serial.py would shadow
the pyserial package it depends on.)

Mirrors the shape of bms_monitor's stream(): a reader loop that survives
the device unplugging, plus a thread-safe write(). Callbacks:

  on_line(text)                one decoded line from the device
                               (decode(raw_bytes) -> text; default UTF-8)
  on_status(connected, msg)    link transitions only (never spammed)
"""

import threading
import time

import serial

DEFAULT_DEVICE = '/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Serial-if00-port0'
DEFAULT_BAUD = 115200
RECONNECT_DELAY = 3.0
READ_TIMEOUT = 1.0


def _decode_utf8(raw):
    return raw.decode('utf-8', errors='replace').strip()


class SerialLink:

    def __init__(self, device=DEFAULT_DEVICE, baud=DEFAULT_BAUD,
                 on_line=None, on_status=None, decode=None):
        self.device = device
        self.baud = baud
        self.on_line = on_line or (lambda line: None)
        self.on_status = on_status or (lambda connected, msg: None)
        self.decode = decode or _decode_utf8
        self._port = None
        self._connected = False
        self._stop = threading.Event()
        self._reset_reason = None
        self._write_lock = threading.Lock()
        self._thread = None

    # -- lifecycle -----------------------------------------------------------

    def start(self):
        """Run the reader/reconnect loop in a daemon thread."""
        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self.run, daemon=True)
        self._thread.start()
        return self._thread

    def stop(self):
        self._stop.set()
        self._close()

    def reset(self, reason):
        """Drop the port and let the loop reopen it.

        For a port that is open but dead - e.g. a stalled USB endpoint, where
        writes still "succeed" into the tty buffer and nothing ever arrives.
        """
        self._reset_reason = reason
        self._close()

    @property
    def connected(self):
        return self._connected

    # -- reader loop -----------------------------------------------------------

    def run(self):
        while not self._stop.is_set():
            try:
                port = serial.Serial(self.device, self.baud,
                                     timeout=READ_TIMEOUT)
            except (serial.SerialException, OSError) as e:
                self._set_status(
                    False, 'cannot open %s (%s), retrying in %.0fs'
                    % (self.device, type(e).__name__, RECONNECT_DELAY))
                self._stop.wait(RECONNECT_DELAY)
                continue

            self._port = port
            self._set_status(True, 'connected to %s @ %d baud'
                             % (self.device, self.baud))
            try:
                while not self._stop.is_set():
                    raw = port.readline()  # b'' on timeout
                    if raw:
                        line = self.decode(raw)
                        if line:
                            self.on_line(line)
            except (serial.SerialException, OSError, TypeError) as e:
                # TypeError: stop() closed the port under readline() and
                # pyserial read from fd None. Expected on shutdown only.
                if self._stop.is_set():
                    break
                reason, self._reset_reason = self._reset_reason, None
                if reason is not None:
                    # reset() closed the port under readline(), on purpose
                    self._set_status(False, 'reset (%s), reopening' % reason)
                elif isinstance(e, TypeError):
                    raise           # a real bug, not a link fault
                else:
                    self._set_status(
                        False, 'link lost (%s), retrying in %.0fs'
                        % (type(e).__name__, RECONNECT_DELAY))
            finally:
                self._close()
            self._stop.wait(RECONNECT_DELAY)

    # -- writing ------------------------------------------------------------

    def write(self, text):
        """Send one line to the device. Returns True on success."""
        return self.write_bytes((text.rstrip('\n') + '\n').encode('utf-8'))

    def write_bytes(self, data):
        """Send raw bytes, unmodified. Returns True on success."""
        with self._write_lock:
            port = self._port
            if port is None or not self._connected:
                return False
            try:
                port.write(data)
                return True
            except (serial.SerialException, OSError, TypeError, ValueError):
                # TypeError/ValueError: the reader closed the port under us
                return False

    # -- helpers ------------------------------------------------------------

    def _close(self):
        # under the write lock so a write never sees a half-closed port
        with self._write_lock:
            port, self._port = self._port, None
        if port is not None:
            try:
                port.close()
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
                pass

    def _set_status(self, connected, message):
        if connected != self._connected:
            self._connected = connected
            self.on_status(connected, message)

===== src/serial_control/serial_control/argus_protocol.py (212 lines)
"""ARGUS DRIVE serial command encoder (firmware v4.1.3).

The console speaks mnemonic tokens on /cmd ("GEAR:D", "ACCEL:40"); the
firmware speaks one line per command at 115200 8N1: the command byte and
0-2 argument bytes written as hex TEXT, then LF - "0x05 01\n". This module
is the only place that knows the mapping. Source: ARGUS DRIVE Serial
Command Interface Specification, firmware v4.1.3, doc rev 1.0.

The spec says "binary/hex"; on the vehicle (2026-10-04) raw bytes were
answered "0x00;ERR;BAD_FORMAT" while the text "0x01" got
"0x01;OK;040103". Replies are text too: "0x<cmd>;<OK|ERR>;<data>".
Arguments follow a space, as in the spec's examples: "0x07 02" was
answered "0x07;OK;02" on the vehicle.

Only the operator vocabulary is reachable. Admin actuator control
(0x12/0x13), reset (0x0C), Wi-Fi (0x0D/0x0E), limit writes (0x0F/0x10 with
arguments) and limit enable (0x11) are deliberately absent, so no browser
client can bypass the steering limits or reconfigure the module.

  Token            Frame (text) Meaning
  INFO             01           firmware version
  ACCEL:<0-100>    02 nn        throttle %, firmware clamps duty to 20-90%
  READ:BRAKE       03           brake feedback, 0.1% units
  READ:STEER       04           steering angle, 0.1 deg units
  STEER:L|R|STOP   05 01|02|00  steering actuator (limits + stall enforced)
  BRAKE:APPLY|RELEASE|STOP
                   06 01|02|00  brake actuator fwd / rev / stop
  SPEED:1|2|3      07 0n        speed mode
  GEAR:D|N|R       08 01|02|03
  LIGHT:OFF|RING|HIGH|LOW
                   09 00-03     one light at a time
  IND:OFF|L|R|PARK 0A 00-03     indicators share one channel with parking
  HORN             0B 01        0.5 s pulse (longer pulses are locked)
  READ:BRKLIM      0F           read brake limits
  READ:STRLIM      10           read steering limits
  STEER:C          14           auto-centre, reply arrives asynchronously
  STATUS           16           all switch states
  ESTOP            02 00, 06 01, 05 00

MODE:*, NAV:* and AUTO:* belong to the console's autonomy layer and have
no firmware equivalent; they encode to no frames.
"""

TERMINATOR = b'\n'

# console-only namespaces: accepted, nothing goes on the wire
_CONSOLE_ONLY = ('MODE:', 'NAV:', 'AUTO:')

_FIXED = {
    'INFO': (0x01,),
    'READ:BRAKE': (0x03,),
    'READ:STEER': (0x04,),
    'STEER:STOP': (0x05, 0x00),
    'STEER:L': (0x05, 0x01),
    'STEER:R': (0x05, 0x02),
    'BRAKE:STOP': (0x06, 0x00),
    'BRAKE:APPLY': (0x06, 0x01),
    'BRAKE:RELEASE': (0x06, 0x02),
    'SPEED:1': (0x07, 0x01),
    'SPEED:2': (0x07, 0x02),
    'SPEED:3': (0x07, 0x03),
    'GEAR:D': (0x08, 0x01),
    'GEAR:N': (0x08, 0x02),
    'GEAR:R': (0x08, 0x03),
    'LIGHT:OFF': (0x09, 0x00),
    'LIGHT:RING': (0x09, 0x01),
    'LIGHT:HIGH': (0x09, 0x02),
    'LIGHT:LOW': (0x09, 0x03),
    'IND:OFF': (0x0A, 0x00),
    'IND:L': (0x0A, 0x01),
    'IND:R': (0x0A, 0x02),
    'IND:PARK': (0x0A, 0x03),
    'HORN': (0x0B, 0x01),
    'READ:BRKLIM': (0x0F,),
    'READ:STRLIM': (0x10,),
    'STEER:C': (0x14,),
    'STATUS': (0x16,),
}

# Throttle to zero first (harmless if braking already), then brake (which
# also locks throttle at 0%), then stop the steering actuator mid-move.
_ESTOP = ((0x02, 0x00), (0x06, 0x01), (0x05, 0x00))


class UnknownCommand(ValueError):
    """The token is not in the operator vocabulary."""


def _frame(cmd, *args):
    text = '0x%02X' % cmd + ''.join(' %02X' % a for a in args)
    return text.encode('ascii') + TERMINATOR


def _accel(arg):
    # isascii: '²'.isdigit() is True but int('²') raises
    if not (arg.isascii() and arg.isdigit()) or not 0 <= int(arg) <= 100:
        raise UnknownCommand('ACCEL takes an integer 0-100, got %r' % arg)
    return [_frame(0x02, int(arg))]


def encode(token):
    """Translate one console token into a list of firmware frames.

    Returns [] for console-only tokens. Raises UnknownCommand for
    anything outside the vocabulary.
    """
    key = str(token).strip().upper()
    if key in _FIXED:
        return [_frame(*_FIXED[key])]
    if key == 'ESTOP':
        return [_frame(*body) for body in _ESTOP]
    if key.startswith('ACCEL:'):
        return _accel(key[len('ACCEL:'):])
    if key.startswith(_CONSOLE_ONLY):
        return []
    raise UnknownCommand('not an ARGUS DRIVE command: %r' % token)


def describe(frames):
    """Frames for logs, terminators omitted: '0x05 01 | 0x05 00'."""
    return ' | '.join(f.rstrip(TERMINATOR).decode('ascii') for f in frames)


def parse_reply(line):
    """'0x01;OK;040103' -> ('0x01', 'OK', '040103'); None if not a reply."""
    parts = line.split(';', 2)
    if len(parts) < 2 or not parts[0].lower().startswith('0x'):
        return None
    return (parts[0], parts[1], parts[2] if len(parts) > 2 else '')


def firmware_version(data):
    """INFO reply data '040103' -> '4.1.3'; None if it isn't 6 digits."""
    if len(data) != 6 or not data.isdigit():
        return None
    return '.'.join(str(int(data[i:i + 2])) for i in (0, 2, 4))


def format_reply(raw):
    """One line read from the firmware, as text if printable, else hex."""
    body = raw.rstrip(b'\r\n')
    try:
        text = body.decode('ascii')
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        text = None
    if text is not None and text.isprintable():
        return text.strip()
    return 'hex: ' + body.hex(' ')


# -- steering feedback --------------------------------------------------------
# READ:STEER replies "0x04;OK;05D9": one hex word in 0.1 deg (149.7).
# READ:STRLIM replies "0x10;OK;029E097E": min then max, same units.

def _hex_word(text):
    if not isinstance(text, str) or len(text) != 4:
        return None
    try:
        return int(text, 16)
    except ValueError:
        return None


def steer_angle_deg(data):
    """READ:STEER reply data -> absolute angle in degrees, or None."""
    raw = _hex_word(data)
    return None if raw is None else raw / 10.0


def steer_limits_deg(data):
    """READ:STRLIM reply data -> (min, max) degrees, or None."""
    if not isinstance(data, str) or len(data) != 8:
        return None
    lo, hi = _hex_word(data[:4]), _hex_word(data[4:])
    if lo is None or hi is None or lo >= hi:
        return None
    return lo / 10.0, hi / 10.0


def relative_steer(angle, limits, invert=False):
    """Absolute angle -> degrees from centre, None without limits.

    Centre is the middle of the configured limits - the same point the
    firmware's own STEER:C (0x14) centres to. Positive is whichever way the
    angle grows; invert flips it so positive means right.
    """
    if limits is None:
        return None
    offset = angle - (limits[0] + limits[1]) / 2.0
    return -offset if invert else offset


# -- brake feedback -----------------------------------------------------------
# Since the 4.1.4 firmware READ:BRAKE replies like READ:STEER: "0x03;OK;0457",
# one hex word in 0.1 deg. READ:BRKLIM replies min then max, like 0x10.

brake_angle_deg = steer_angle_deg
brake_limits_deg = steer_limits_deg


def brake_percent(angle, limits, invert=False):
    """Brake angle -> 0-100 % of travel between the limits, None without.

    0 % is at the min limit and 100 % at the max; invert flips that if the
    actuator's angle shrinks as the brake applies. Clamped to 0-100.
    """
    if limits is None:
        return None
    pct = (angle - limits[0]) / (limits[1] - limits[0]) * 100.0
    if invert:
        pct = 100.0 - pct
    return max(0.0, min(100.0, pct))

===== src/serial_control/serial_control/accel_protocol.py (121 lines)
"""Accelerator board encoder, and the router that splits /cmd tokens
between the two serial links.

Throttle moved off the ARGUS DRIVE controller onto its own board: a
separate serial device that drives the accelerator PWM on GPIO9. Its
protocol is ARGUS-shaped hex text, but arguments follow a semicolon, not a
space:

  CMD   NAME   ARGS                      REPLY
  0x01  INFO   none                      0x01;OK;AC0102
  0x02  ACCEL  duty % as hex byte 00-64  0x02;OK;<duty hex>
                                         0x02;ERR;INVALID_DUTY
  other                                  ERR;UNKNOWN_COMMAND

Frames: "0x01\\n", "0x02;32\\n" (50%). The board also accepts "01" and
"02;32"; we always send the 0x form.

The ARGUS firmware used to zero the throttle when braking and to reject
ACCEL while the brake was on. The accel board cannot see the brake, so
route() now enforces that rule on the host:

  ACCEL:n        accel board only; refused while braking or while the
                 drive link is down (unless n == 0)
  BRAKE:APPLY    duty 0 to the accel board first, then brake; locks throttle
  BRAKE:RELEASE  release brake; unlocks throttle
  ESTOP          duty 0 to the accel board first, then the ARGUS ESTOP
  anything else  ARGUS drive link, unchanged (argus_protocol.encode)
"""

from collections import namedtuple

from serial_control import argus_protocol
from serial_control.argus_protocol import TERMINATOR, UnknownCommand

INFO = b'0x01' + TERMINATOR

# The ARGUS firmware clamped duty to 90%; keep that ceiling by default.
DEFAULT_MAX_PCT = 90

Route = namedtuple('Route', 'main accel brake_on')


class ThrottleLocked(ValueError):
    """ACCEL above 0 was requested while the brake is applied."""


def duty_frame(pct):
    """Throttle percent 0-100 -> '0x02;<hex>\\n'."""
    pct = int(pct)
    if not 0 <= pct <= 100:
        raise ValueError('duty must be 0-100, got %d' % pct)
    return ('0x02;%02X' % pct).encode('ascii') + TERMINATOR


ZERO = duty_frame(0)


def firmware_version(data):
    """INFO reply data 'AC0102' -> '1.2'; None for any other shape."""
    if len(data) != 6 or data[:2].upper() != 'AC' or not data[2:].isdigit():
        return None
    return '%d.%d' % (int(data[2:4]), int(data[4:6]))


def route(token, brake_on, max_pct=DEFAULT_MAX_PCT, drive_up=True):
    """Split one console token into frames for each link.

    Returns Route(main, accel, brake_on): frames for the ARGUS drive link,
    frames for the accel board (send these first), and the brake state
    after this command. drive_up False means the brake is unreachable.
    Raises UnknownCommand or ThrottleLocked.
    """
    key = str(token).strip().upper()
    if key.startswith('ACCEL:'):
        # argus_protocol validates the range and the integer form
        argus_protocol.encode(key)
        pct = int(key[len('ACCEL:'):])
        if brake_on and pct > 0:
            raise ThrottleLocked('ACCEL:%d refused: brake is applied' % pct)
        if not drive_up and pct > 0:
            raise ThrottleLocked('ACCEL:%d refused: drive link is down' % pct)
        return Route([], [duty_frame(min(pct, max_pct))], brake_on)

    main = argus_protocol.encode(key)
    if key in ('ESTOP', 'BRAKE:APPLY'):
        return Route(main, [ZERO], True)
    if key == 'BRAKE:RELEASE':
        return Route(main, [], False)
    return Route(main, [], brake_on)


class AckWatch:
    """Detects a silent accel board.

    The board answers every frame ("0x02;OK;..", "0x01;OK;.."). A USB-serial
    link can stall (kernel "urb stopped: -32") while host writes still
    succeed into the tty buffer, so a write returning True proves nothing;
    only a reply does. dead() is True once the oldest unanswered write is
    older than timeout_s.
    """

    def __init__(self, timeout_s=1.0):
        self.timeout_s = timeout_s
        self._oldest = None

    def sent(self, now):
        if self._oldest is None:
            self._oldest = now

    def replied(self, now):
        self._oldest = None

    def reset(self):
        self._oldest = None

    def dead(self, now):
        return self._oldest is not None and now - self._oldest > self.timeout_s


__all__ = ['AckWatch', 'INFO', 'ZERO', 'DEFAULT_MAX_PCT', 'Route', 'ThrottleLocked',
           'UnknownCommand', 'duty_frame', 'firmware_version', 'route']

===== README
# ros2_new

ROS 2 Humble workspace for the Remo vehicle: a browser operator console backed
by a BLE battery monitor, a serial drive-by-wire bridge, a live log feed and an
on-request node health check.

Target platform is a **Jetson AGX Orin on JetPack 6.x (Ubuntu 22.04)**, running
Humble **natively** — Ubuntu 22.04 is Humble's tier-1 platform, so no container
is required. `run_container.sh` remains for a throwaway or reproducible
environment.

## Packages

| Package | Node(s) | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `web_server` | `web_server` | Serves the console and one authenticated WebSocket. Frames are namespace-multiplexed (`/bms` `/cmd` `/api` `/alert` `/terminal`) and bridged to ROS: browser → `~/<ns>/out`, `~/<ns>/in` → browser. |
| `bms_monitor` | `bms`, `bms_listener` | Polls a Xiaoxiang/JBD pack over BLE, publishes snapshots, and runs an edge-triggered alert engine. |
| `serial_control` | `cmd` | Translates `/cmd` console tokens into ARGUS DRIVE firmware v4.1.3 hex-text frames (`0x05 01`) (`argus_protocol.py`) on the CH340 serial link, with link/write/device alerts. Throttle (`ACCEL:n`) goes to a separate accelerator board on its own serial link (`accel_device`, default the FT232R `A5069RR4`; frames `0x02;<duty hex>`, `accel_protocol.py`), capped at `accel_max_pct` (90). The node enforces the brake interlock: `BRAKE:APPLY`, `ESTOP`, drive-link loss and every accel-board (re)connect send duty 0, and `ACCEL` is refused while braking. Polls the steering angle (`0x04`, 5 Hz) and publishes it on `/cmd/steer_angle` (absolute) and `/cmd/steer` (degrees from centre), and to the console. Brake feedback is read the same way (`0x03` polled, limits `0x0F` on connect) and published on `/cmd/brake_angle` (deg) and `/cmd/brake` (0–100 % between the limits; `brake_invert` flips it). Admin, reset, Wi-Fi and limit commands are not reachable from the console. |
| `system_terminal` | `system_terminal` | Forwards `/rosout` to the `/terminal` namespace. |
| `status_check` | `status_server`, `status_bridge` | On-request node liveness/health via a ROS action, exposed on `/api`. |
| `status_interfaces` | — | The `CheckStatus` action definition. |
| `zed_bringup` | `detection_bridge`, `depth_colorizer` | Runs the ZED wrapper against the two live ZED X cameras on the GMSL link (or recorded SVOs), with neural depth and object detection, and bridges detections to the `/camera` namespace. |
| `phone_gps` | `gps` | Turns the `/gps` namespace's browser geolocation frames into `sensor_msgs/NavSatFix` on `/gps/fix`. |
| `mobile_remote` | `mobile_remote` | Phone remote served on its own port, **8770**. The live RGB camera (web_video_server on 8080, front by default, switchable to rear) fills the background behind the controls; no detections or other perception. A static React app in `frontend/` that opens the same authenticated WebSocket as the console on 8765, so it uses the same `/cmd` path and safety rules. Throttle is hold-to-drive (lifting the finger sends 0); brake is hold-to-apply (lifting holds the actuator, so the brake stays on and throttle stays locked until Release brake); steering is hold-to-move; there is no E-STOP button; two per-phone switches (off by default): Auto centre sends STEER:C when a steering pad is let go, Auto release sends BRAKE:RELEASE instead of holding when the brake pedal is lifted; hiding, leaving or blurring the page sends throttle 0 and steering stop. Full screen: the in-app button (Android), or Add to Home Screen (iPhone). It reuses the console's auth, command tokens and gauges via the `@console` alias. `npm test` covers the throttle logic. |

## Setup

### 1. ROS 2 Humble

Install `ros-humble-desktop` from `packages.ros.org` following the [official
Humble instructions](https://docs.ros.org/en/humble/Installation/Ubuntu-Install-Debs.html),
plus the build tooling:

```bash
sudo apt install ros-humble-desktop python3-colcon-common-extensions python3-rosdep
sudo rosdep init && rosdep update
```

Substitute `ros-humble-ros-base` if you do not want `rviz2`/`rqt` on the board.

### 2. Dependencies

```bash
cd ~/Desktop/ros2_new
rosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -r -y
```

That covers `python3-serial`. **Two dependencies rosdep cannot satisfy on
jammy must then come from pip:**

```bash
pip3 install --user -r requirements.txt
```

This step is not optional, and skipping it fails in a way that looks like a
bug in this code rather than a missing dependency:

- **`websockets`** — jammy ships 9.1, three majors behind. On 9.1 the node
  starts, logs `serving UI + WebSocket`, accepts the TCP connection, and then
  never answers plain HTTP. The browser hangs with nothing in the log.
  Pinned to 12.0.
- **`bleak`** — no jammy package exists at all, so rosdep resolves it to
  nothing and installs silently. Pinned to 3.0.2.

If these nodes will ever run as a systemd service or as root, `--user` installs
where that account cannot see them; use a venv or a system-wide install
instead.

BLE access goes through the host BlueZ stack over D-Bus, which needs no setup
natively. Serial access needs the account in the group owning the CH340 device —
on L4T that is `gpio`, not `dialout`.

### 3. ZED camera (optional)

Needs the ZED SDK (5.4.x) already installed under `/usr/local/zed`. The ROS 2
wrapper is not an apt package, so it is cloned into `src/` at the tag matching
the SDK — `.gitignore` excludes it rather than nesting a second git repo:

```bash
cd src
git clone --recursive --branch v5.4.1 --depth 1 \
    https://github.com/stereolabs/zed-ros2-wrapper.git
cd ..
rosdep install --from-paths src --ignore-src -r -y
sudo apt install -y ros-humble-web-video-server
```

If the build stops at `Specify CUDA_TOOLKIT_ROOT_DIR`, install
`nvidia-jetpack nvidia-jetpack-dev`, or pass
`--cmake-args -DCUDA_TOOLKIT_ROOT_DIR=/usr/local/cuda`.

The SDK's AI models download on first use and are then TensorRT-optimized for
the specific board and resolution, which takes minutes per model. Sticking to
`NEURAL_LIGHT`/`NEURAL` depth and the `MULTI_CLASS_BOX_*` detectors reuses what
is already optimized; `NEURAL_PLUS` pays that cost again.

### 4. Build

```bash
source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
colcon build
source install/setup.bash
```

### 5. Console (optional)

The React console needs Node 20+. Without it, `web_server` falls back to the
legacy diagnostics UI at `/` — a designed fallback, not a failure.

Node 20 is installed under `~/.local/lib/nodejs` with symlinks in
`~/.local/bin`, so it needs no root. Ubuntu's default `~/.profile` puts
`~/.local/bin` on `PATH` at login; in an existing shell, export it first:

```bash
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
cd src/web_server/frontend
cp .env.example .env   # then set VITE_MAPTILER_KEY (see below)
npm ci                 # npm install if you are changing dependencies
npm run build          # -> frontend/dist, picked up by colcon build
```

