===== work_doc
# Steering-sensor mechanism & Orin enclosure — work documentation

Compiled 2026-09-19 from the project sources and the session transcripts in
`~/.claude/projects/-home-tessy-01-Desktop/`.

---

## 1. Brief

Two printable hardware designs were built on this machine, both parametric
OpenSCAD, both verified by scripted 3D interference checks rather than by eye.

**A. `~/Desktop/steer_mech` — a 6:1 absolute steering-angle sensor.**
A gear clamps onto the vehicle's existing ~20 mm steering pipe. Six turns of the
pipe give one turn of a planetary output, and a magnet in that output is read by
an encoder board held on the one member that never rotates. Reading *after* the
reduction is the point: one output revolution is one absolute cycle, so the
steering angle is unambiguous across the full range. Built today: a **stand**
that carries the whole head off the vehicle's existing 3-bore mounting face, a
snap-on **cover** with an integrated ESP32-S3 bay, and a full assembly preview set.

**B. `~/Desktop/orin_case` — a two-part case for the Jetson AGX Orin devkit.**
Reworked today to fit a 177 mm print bed (was 187 mm, unsliceable) and to open a
12 mm accessory bay under the devkit for a GMSL card and its ZED coax connector.

**Status.** Both are rendered, manifold, checked and exported to STL/STEP. Two
things are open and stated in §6: the Orin case is **93.65 mm**, above the 90 mm
ceiling that was set, and `steer_mech` has **no git commit yet**.

| Deliverable | File | Size |
|---|---|---|
| Sensor stand | `steer_mech/out/stand.stl` | 157.1 × 105.6 × 33.0 mm, 89.6 cm³ |
| Cover | `steer_mech/out/case.stl` | 158.3 × 109.4 × 98.2 mm, 85.0 cm³ |
| Cover lid | `steer_mech/out/case_lid.stl` | 65.0 × 40.2 × 7.0 mm, 5.4 cm³ |
| Orin lower | `orin_case/lower.stl` / `.step` | 173.0 × 146.0 × 50.0 mm, 176 cm³ |
| Orin upper | `orin_case/upper.stl` / `.step` | 146.0 × 146.0 × 51.7 mm, 96 cm³ |

---

## 2. Project A — steer_mech

### 2.1 The drive

```
pipe (Ø20, offset 50 mm from the output axis)
  -> split clamp gear, 40T        two glued halves around the pipe
  -> 1:1 external mesh            no ratio, only reach
  -> 60T input gear + 18T sun     one compound part (split in two to assemble)
  -> planetary, ring FIXED        4:1
  -> carrier output at the centre
       |- Ø10 magnet in the rotating output boss
       '- encoder board on a bracket bolted to the fixed ring
```

Module 1.0, 20° pressure angle, 10 mm face. Sun 17T, planets 4 × 17T, ring 51T,
pair 40T → 60T. Ratio is exact: `(1 + 51/17) × (60/40) = 4.000 × 1.500 = 6.000`.

Three design conclusions are forced by geometry, not preference, and each is
asserted in `src/params.scad`:

- **The pipe cannot pass through the centre.** A Ø20 pipe wants ~26 mm of hub;
  the planets reach in to Ø24 at the gear plane. Boring the sun to 20 mm leaves
  a 1.62 mm wall in a part that is itself split. Offsetting keeps the verified
  planetary untouched.
- **The ring must be fixed, not driven.** Ring-driven with a centre output gives
  `1 + S/R` (always between 1 and 2) or `S/R` (the centre spins *faster*).
  6:1 inward in one concentric stage is impossible.
- **The 6:1 is split 4:1 × 1.5:1.** All-planetary made the first version 212 mm
  wide; the split brought the ring from Ø150.8 to Ø69.5 and the overall width to
  106 mm. The cost is tooth bending strength (~0.44× from module 1.5 → 1.0);
  widen `face_width` before touching the module.

Everything is pinned with glued Ø4 / Ø2 smooth dowel — no threads anywhere in
the gearbox. Holes are Ø4.25 / Ø2.2, a ~0.12 mm radial glue gap, asserted to
stay between 0.1 and 0.5 mm.

### 2.2 The stand — `src/stand.scad` → `out/stand.stl`

Carries the whole head off the mounting face already on the vehicle.

| Host feature | Value |
|---|---|
| Bore spacing | 66.5 mm centre-to-centre (measured 6.6–6.7 cm) |
| Bolt circle | r 38.39 mm (circumradius of that triangle) |
| Central cut | **Ø57.30 mm** — from an 18 cm *circumference*, 180/π |
| Screws | M6 assumed, Ø6.6 clearance |

The 18 cm reading matters: as a circumference it leaves 9.7 mm of flange out to
each bore, which is a normal flange. As a *diameter* it would swallow the bolt
circle entirely and the bores could not exist. All five host numbers sit under
`MEASURED` at the top of the file and every one is asserted.

**Shape, and why:**

- **Flat face.** Everything glued to the rod sweeps Ø47.7 — the clamp-hub
  corners, which stick out further than the teeth. The hub is glued at whatever
  clock angle the fitter leaves it, so the stand treats it as round and stops
  dead 3 mm short at one vertical plane.
- **1.8 mm shell, straight-walled.** 1.8 rather than 2.0 because at a 0.4 mm
  nozzle that is exactly 4 perimeters — solid walls, no infill, lightest and
  stiffest per gram. Straight follows from that: at the original 30° splay the
  screw gussets needed 44 mm of height inside a 25 mm pedestal.
- **Four gussets, no collar.** A ring reaching a screw at r 38 would be 13 mm of
  solid all the way round. Four 14 mm gussets do the same for four holes. The
  top 12.2 mm of each is square before the 45° fall-away — sloped from the top,
  the line crosses the screw hole ~5 mm down and the screw ends in fresh air.
- **The plate keeps an 11 mm web.** It is a cantilever from three bolts to the
  pedestal; the bending moment peaks exactly where the lightening opening and
  the rod bore want to meet. Left alone they merge and the arm hangs on two
  thin necks.
- **A 1.2 mm locating pocket** shaped to the gearbox base plate. It locates in
  x, y *and* rotation, and the arms at 90/150/210/270° are not 4-fold
  symmetric, so it cannot go on a quarter-turn out.

**Fixing — 4 × M4 from the top** (`fix_mode = "screw" | "insert" | "dowel"`).
Each screw replaces that ring hole's glued dowel and clamps ring + base leg +
base plate + stand in one go, through holes that already exist. Nothing is
re-drilled. M4 × 45 at 90/210°, M4 × 60 at 150/270°, into Ø3.4 × 8 mm
self-tapping blind holes.

**Before printing:** the rod must project **65 mm** above the mounting face —
that is where the pipe gear's top face has to sit. There is no adjustment in
the part. Pedestal 25 mm (`ped_h_set`), 33 mm overall, plate down, no support.

### 2.3 The cover — `src/case.scad` → `out/case.stl` + `out/case_lid.stl`

A shroud, not a housing: nothing is located by it and nothing bears on it.

- **Wall 1.5 mm** — read from "0.15 cm". 0.15 mm was flagged as unprintable
  (one extrusion is ~0.4 mm). Note 1.5 is 3.75 perimeters at 0.4; print at a
  0.5 mm extrusion width, or set `case_wall = 1.6`.
- **Roof opening r 28 mm is not a clearance hole.** It is sized against the
  Ø47.7 sweep of the glued clamp hub. Anything smaller and the cover could
  never be lifted off the rod once the gear is fitted — it would have to be cut
  off. This was a real defect in the first version, caught by the r 25–30
  requirement.
- **Snap lock.** Three posts on the plate rim cam aside as the wall passes, then
  spring into three windows. The barbs' undersides are flat, so pulling up only
  tightens them. To open: press a barb flush, lift that corner, three corners
  one at a time. Plus a Ø9 cable gland at 210° (not 180° — that is the USB end)
  and three 9 × 2.5 mm drain notches, because a sealed box on a vehicle collects
  condensation.
- **ESP32-S3 bay**, in the 6 mm of dead air that already existed between the
  encoder board and the roof — the footprint does not grow. Built to the actual
  board in `out/robu.avif`: bare PCB, 55 × 28 × 1.6 mm, 3.6 mm module can,
  1.2 mm solder tails, twin USB-C on one end behind a 26 × 7.5 mm letterbox.
  Two clips hold it, four standoffs carry it, and the encoder tail comes up
  through a 14 × 5 mm slot in the bay floor. Lid finishes flush with the roof —
  from outside there is nothing but a panel line. Lift the lid, lift the board
  out; no fasteners.

