-- /home/tessy_01/Desktop/steer_mech/README.md
# steer_mech

Parametric 6:1 reduction drive, written in OpenSCAD.

A gear clamps onto an existing ~20 mm pipe. Turn the pipe 6 times, the centre
output turns once.

> **Full documentation is in [docs/INDEX.md](docs/INDEX.md)** — design, per-file
> parts reference, every parameter and assertion, assembly order and BOM, the
> test harnesses, the stand and the cover. Where this README and those documents
> disagree, `src/params.scad` is the source of truth and
> [docs/DRIFT.md](docs/DRIFT.md) lists every known difference.

![assembly](docs/assembly.png)

![layout](docs/layout_top.png)

![encoder bracket](docs/encoder.png)

## How the drive is laid out

```
pipe (20 mm, offset)
  -> split clamp gear, 60T          two halves, bolted around the pipe
  -> 1:1 external mesh              no ratio, just reach
  -> 60T input gear + 18T sun       one compound part
  -> planetary, ring FIXED          6:1
  -> carrier output at the centre   1 turn
       |- magnet in the boss end face     rotates
       '- encoder board on a bracket      stationary, bolted to the ring

sun_input is carried from below by the base, also bolted to the ring
```

**All of the 6:1 comes from the planetary.** The external pair is 1:1 and exists
only to move the pipe off the centre axis.

## Why the pipe is offset, not through the middle

It cannot go through the middle. A 20 mm pipe needs about 26 mm of hub around
it, but the planets reach in to 24 mm diameter at the gear plane. Boring the
sun out to 20 mm leaves a 1.62 mm wall, and the sun is split in half, so that
wall is really two thin shells. Forcing it would mean module 2.0 and a 192 mm
ring gear.

Offsetting the pipe keeps the verified 147 mm planetary untouched.

## Why the ring is fixed, not driven

Driving the outer ring and taking output at the centre **cannot** give 6:1.
This is a kinematic limit, not a design choice:

| fixed | input | output | input turns per 1 output |
|---|---|---|---|
| ring | sun | carrier | **6.000** ← used here |
| sun | ring | carrier | 1.200 |
| carrier | ring | sun | 0.200 (centre spins *faster*) |

With the ring driving and a centre output there are only two options. Sun fixed
gives `1 + S/R`; since `R = S + 2P`, the ring is always larger than the sun, so
-- /home/tessy_01/Desktop/steer_mech/docs/DRIFT.md
# Documentation drift register

Comments and README passages that no longer match the code, found while
compiling this documentation on 2026-09-19. **`src/params.scad` is the source of
truth** — where anything disagrees with it, params wins.

None of these affect a rendered part. They are all stale prose, mostly left over
from the pre-split design (module 1.5, 60T pipe gear, bolted clamp) and from the
bolts-to-glued-dowels change. Verify before trusting any of the passages below.

## In the sources

| File:line | Says | Actually |
|---|---|---|
| `src/params.scad:10` | `ratio = 1 + teeth_ring/teeth_sun = 1 + 90/18 = 6.000` | teeth are 17/17/51 → the **planetary is 4.000**; the 6.000 overall comes from the 1.5:1 pair |
| `src/planet.scad:1` | "Planet gear — **3 off**" | `n_planets = 4` — print four |
| `src/assembly.scad:5–7` | "split clamp gear, **60T**", "**1:1** external mesh", "60T input gear" | pipe gear is **40T**, the pair is **1.5:1**, the input gear is 60T |
| `src/pipe_gear.scad:6` | "the halves **bolt** together and pinch the pipe; drive is by **friction**, so tighten them properly" | two glued Ø4 cross dowels; drive is by the **adhesive bond**. There is nothing to tighten |
| `src/pipe_gear.scad:12` | "miss the **147 mm** ring gear" | ring OD is **69.5 mm** |
| `src/pipe_gear.scad:13` | "90 mm centre distance… both gears **60T at module 1.5**" | centre distance **50 mm**, 40T:60T at **module 1.0** |

## In README.md

| Line | Says | Actually |
|---|---|---|
| 40, 321, 491 | "the **147 mm** ring gear / planetary" | ring OD **69.5 mm** |
| 156 | "`enc_hole_inset` is **1.25 mm**, as specified" | `enc_hole_inset = 1.4` |
| 170, 180 | journal post "**Ø5.8 × 14** into the Ø6 bore" | `base_post_d = 4.8`, `base_post_h = 25` (raised from 14, which left the sun end cantilevered 13 mm), into the Ø5 `sun_bore` |
| 177 | base legs "4 off, **doglegged**" | straight. The same README says later that outboard lugs removed the dogleg — the table was not updated |
| 179 | leg radius "38 mm at the bottom, **stepping to 30.75** above z = −4" | straight at **r 38** throughout |
| 253 | ring hole 330° "**left free** for chassis mounting" | the BOM emits a **Ø4 × 10 mm** pin for it |
| 254 | pipe clamp pins "Ø4 × **30.0 mm**" | `clamp_pin_len = hub_y` = **26 mm** |
| 280 | "`split_gap` **dropped from 0.6 mm to 0.2 mm**" | `split_gap = 0.6` in the tree. Either the change was reverted or it was never made |
| 375 | section "Why the input is **two parts, not one**" | `sun_input.scad` builds it as **one part**, and its own header explains why splitting it does not help. What makes it assemblable is the build **order**, not the part count — see [ASSEMBLY.md](ASSEMBLY.md) |

## In docs/STAND.md

| Line | Says | Actually |
|---|---|---|
| 88–92 | "The pedestal is a truncated **cone**, wide at the bottom (r 52.8) and narrowing to r 43 at the top… a **30 deg wall**" | `ped_slope = 0` — the wall is **straight**. The paragraph immediately below it ("Straight is a consequence of that") is the current one; this one is left from before the gussets |

## Corrected here

`docs/STAND.md` stated 145.9 × 96.0 mm and 72 cm³. The part grew when the
cover's snap posts were added to the plate rim; it now measures **157.1 × 105.6
× 33.0 mm, 89.6 cm³ (~111 g PLA)**. Corrected in place on 2026-09-19.

## How to re-check

The BOM and the derived geometry both come out of `params.scad` itself, so they
cannot drift:

```
openscad -o /tmp/x.stl src/params.scad 2>&1 | grep '^ECHO' | sed 's/ECHO: //; s/"//g'
```

Measured STL extents, which is what the tables in these docs quote:

```
awk '/vertex/{x=$2+0;y=$3+0;z=$4+0; if(n++==0){ax=bx=x;ay=by=y;az=bz=z}

